foreign  welcome back to Harry Potter Theory  today we're going to be discussing a  question that I'm sure is at the  Forefront of many Harry Potter fans  Minds who is more powerful Voldemort or  Dumbledore Voldemort and Dumbledore are  the yin and yang of wizards each  extremely powerful and a strong  representative of the classic  relationship between good and evil each  one acting as a symbol for the  invariable struggle between light and  darkness we know how Harry Potter ends  we know that Voldemort is unsuccessful  in securing a shroud of Darkness over  the world but that doesn't mean that he  couldn't have been the most powerful  wizard of all time and the fact that  light prevailed doesn't mean that  Dumbledore was the most powerful wizard  either we've got to look deeper many  assume that because Voldemort ultimately  lost and because Dumbledore is good that  Dumbledore is the more powerful figure  however this is not a sound argument  whether your good or evil has nothing to  do with your inherent power from an  extremely young age Tom Riddle showcased  intensely powerful innate magical  abilities he was a magical Prodigy if  you will as he grew older and he began  to harness his abilities riddle became  unimaginably powerful performing Feats  of magic that people thought previously  impossible and he knew it too  proclaiming to Harry in the air of  Southern that he was the greatest  sorcerer in the world to which Harry  retorts Dumbledore is the greatest  sorcerer in the world reminding riddle  of Dumbledore and subsequently angering  him in my view this anger showed  insecurity in riddle Harry's statement  reminding him of Dumbledore's power  forced him to consider his own magical  ability in relation to Dumbledore's I  think that he a knows that Dumbledore  was more powerful and became angry  because he resented or B is just  completely unsure of whether or not  Dumbledore is more powerful and face it  as a possibility while Voldemort showed  extreme magical prowess from a young age  he was not alone Dumbledore's magical  abilities consistently turned heads at  Hogwarts as well particularly his skills  surrounding charms and Transfiguration  he also did exceptionally well on his  nudes and Griselda marchbanks a  professor working for the Wizarding  examinations Authority remarked that  Albus was able to do things with a wand  that she had never seen before not only  that but Dumbledore befriended  Grindelwald at a very young age and I  believe that this had a profound impact  on Dumbledore's skill the two spent lots  of time together practicing magic and  Grindelwald was known to have come from  a powerful magical bloodline his great  aunt batilde bagshot publishing a  history of magic a magic Book used to  teach students at Hogwarts Dumbledore  who always outshone everyone magically  was challenged by another for the first  time and I believed that this resulted  in even greater power for the both of  them so we know that both of them were  powerful right from the start showing an  innate capability for magic and an  affinity for learning new skills and  abilities so where does this leave us  how can we definitively determine who  was more powerful Dumbledore was known  for being all too modest and in the  first book there is a moment where I  believe it is partially suggested that  Dumbledore is the more powerful sorcerer  or if not more powerful possessing the  inherent capability to be more powerful  if he so desired McGonagall says to  Dumbledore but you're different everyone  knows you're the only one you know oh  all right Voldemort was frightened of  you flatter me said Dumbledore calmly  Voldemort had powers I will never have  to which McGonagall replies only because  you're too well Noble to use them to me  this hints that perhaps Dumbledore has a  higher overall threshold for power than  Voldemort but then he is perhaps not  willing to make the same sacrifices and  for that reason never achieving quite  the same level of power Voldemort's  level of power was achieved by any means  possible with no exceptions his pursuit  of power was filled with ignorance  showing disregard for the consequences  of the pursuit of dark magic he delved  further into the darkness than anyone  before him and he became powerful  because of it but perhaps at too great a  cost in the pursuit of power he lost  himself  however even though he plunged himself  into magic as much as possible Voldemort  at no point in the books films appears  to exhibit magical ability that far  exceeds or outweighs Dumbledore the only  real point of reference that we have  comes from their duel in the ministry  Atrium but there's also sort of  complicates things further I say this  because the jewel is depicted very  differently from book to film with film  Dumbledore coming across as a bit of a  frail old man and a book Dumbledore  absolutely wiping the floor with  Voldemort when they fight in the  ministry Atrium in the film it's left  very open they actually seem evenly  matched for the most part though I would  argue that Voldemort slightly edges him  out when they meet each other in the  atrium Dumbledore and Voldemort  instantly enter into a magical power  struggle with one flick of their ones  Dumbledore's red jet of light met  Voldemort's green jet in mid-air and the  two held their ground as Spa marks flew  and the room began to Fall to Pieces  unable to contain the sheer magical  power being produced the pair basically  appeared to be evenly matched I'd also  like to add that during their duel in  the film there isn't much talking this  is another point that deviates quite a  lot from the book and I think it is a  piece of evidence that was suggest  Dumbledore's superiority throughout  their duel the books continuously  describe Dumbledore's nonchalant  attitude toward everything going on here  he is faced head to head with one of the  darkest and most powerful wizards of all  time but he's so calm in the passage  describing their duel when he walks  toward Voldemort it's constantly  reinforced that he's calm and that he  had not a fear in the world Dumbledore's  demeanor toward Voldemort during their  duel was even described as though they  were discussing the matter over drinks  the books would lead us to believe that  Dumbledore is far superior to Voldemort  and that Voldemort was completely  outmatched however the there are a few  things to consider here one Dumbledore  was in possession of the Elder Wand the  Elder Wand was the most powerful wand in  existence and it's entirely possible  that this could have accounted for the  difference in power between them in fact  based on how film Voldemort performed  against film Dumbledore I'd venture to  say that film Voldemort is stronger  however it is certainly difficult to  quantify just how much of a boost you  get from the powerful magical artifact  two even book Voldemort had a moment  where he made Dumbledore look a bit  silly look out Harry yelled but even as  he shouted another jet of green light  flew at Dumbledore from Voldemort's wand  and the snake struck Forks swooped down  in front of Dumbledore opened his beak  wide and swallowed the jet of Greenlight  whole He burst into flame and fell to  the floor small wrinkled and flightless  this part is particularly interesting  because it actually actually seems to  paint Dumbledore in a little bit more of  a vulnerable state and I think that it  suggests that Dumbledore was perhaps too  confident at the beginning of the jewel  in the film adaptation at no point to  stumbledore need forks to show up and  save him from one of Voldemort's attacks  but what the text shows us is that had  folks not been there Dumbledore would  have either been hit with the Vada  cadavera or the fiery snakes Bell as  both were being directed at him at once  I feel that this is of particular  importance as it may even indicate the  Dumbledore was the loser of their duel a  Forks blocked a spell from hitting him  and unless Dumbledore really didn't need  folks to do that it suggests that he may  have been the loser but bearing the two  points above in mind we do also have to  remember that both versions of  Dumbledore weren't trying to defeat  Voldemort simply Ward him off I think  that if we had these Wizards Jewel 100  times we'd get some pretty interesting  outcomes particularly if we tossed an  Elder Wand into Voldemort's hands as  well I also think that the answer to  this question sort of goes both ways  I'll give you the film answer and the  book answer the film answer is that  Voldemort is more powerful I say this  because in the film Voldemort appears to  have been slightly edging out Dumbledore  despite Dumbledore's possession of the  Elder Wand but I will add that  Dumbledore wasn't trying to kill him so  it's plausible that a proper duel  between both men would likely result in  a stalemate the book answer is almost  definitively that Dumbledore is more  powerful I say this because even though  Voldemort got a decent shot at  Dumbledore and even though Dumbledore  was saved by folks the book passages are  unwavering in their claims that this  Jewel wasn't a big deal for him I think  that folks save giving him probably  wasn't really necessary and it can  probably be chalked up to a Touch Too  Much overconfidence on Dumbledore's part  however with these two answers in mind I  do have a hot take which is that  Voldemort had he lived longer could have  become more powerful than Dumbledore  Voldemort had access to an entire other  side of the magical Spectrum dark magic  aside The Dumbledore who dabbled in his  youth wouldn't dare delve into again in  this respect Dumbledore is limited  whereas Voldemort had access to anything  and everything this is also based on an  extended timeline where Voldemort gets  to live longer but if Dumbledore  suddenly decided to be evil as well I  think Voldemort would be out of luck and  in reality in the existing timeline  Dumbledore didn't need dark magic he was  able to exhibit unsurpassed magical  ability while remaining entirely himself  and with no sacrifice to his soul  ultimately if we consider the Dumbledore  chose a purer path then I think that we  can reasonably infer that Dumbledore  only scratched the surface of his power  whereas Voldemort was already  capitalizing on pretty much everything  he could this is why I think that  Voldemort always feared Dumbledore the  unknown threshold of Dumbledore's power  this is why the subject of Dumbledore's  power was always a bitter pill for  Voldemort to swallow I hope that you  guys enjoyed the video what do you think  who do you believe is more powerful let  me know down in the comment section  below also for a comparison between  Grindelwald and Voldemort check out my  video which I'll show on screen until  next time remember it matters not what  someone is born but what they grow to be  foreign  [Music]

